Z||000002||fierosound||05-10-2000||12:26 AM||||Gokart...  that link isn't very useful for INTAKE manifolds...<P>Alex...<P>Found this information, but there were no photos.<P><BR>Intake Manifolds: <P>If you are retaining the stock Fiero intake manifolds (there are three pieces), you will need to do some work on each of the manifolds. Be careful! The lower manifold alone costs over $300. <P>Lower Manifold: <P>Using the correct gaskets, blue die and a sharp scribe, make the port matching lines around each port opening. Do all ports on this manifold radius out the injector hole to the port matching line (this will allow a nearly correct cone pattern from the injector and increase air flow remove any casting ridges near the injector holes <BR>match the remaining areas, blending back approx 1/2". <P>NOTE: do not match the areas by the injector holes match the inlet holes at the top of this manifold but be careful this area is very thin <P>Middle Manifold: <P>Match the lower openings try to maintain a smooth airflow transistion into the lower manifold for the top 6 holes, simply remove any casting imperfections <P>The Top Manifold only requires that the casting imprefections be removed at inlet and outlets <P>||||reg||10
Z||000003||Alex4mula||05-10-2000||10:23 AM||||That's what I was looking for. Started already but just wanna be sure I'm doing the right stuff. Thanks guys.||||reg||14
